/**
 * @page SoDaCLI_manual SoDaCLI: Command-Line Interface to SoDaServer
 *
 * @tableofcontents
 *
 * @section sodacli_overview Overview
 *
 * SoDaCLI is a read-eval-print-loop (REPL) that connects to a running
 * SoDaServer process and lets you send and receive @ref SoDa::Command
 * messages from the command line.  It is useful for scripted testing,
 * manual exploration of radio parameters, and regression harnesses.
 *
 * SoDaCLI talks to SoDaServer over the same Unix-domain command socket
 * that the Qt GUI uses.  It can optionally launch SoDaServer itself
 * before connecting.
 *
 * All input typed at the prompt and all messages received from the server
 * are appended to @c CLIHarnessLog.md in the current working directory.
 *
 * @section sodacli_build Building
 *
 * SoDaCLI is built automatically as part of the normal CMake build:
 * @code
 *   cmake -B build .
 *   cmake --build build --target SoDaCLI
 * @endcode
 *
 * The resulting binary is @c build/TestCLI/SoDaCLI.
 *
 * @section sodacli_usage Usage
 *
 * Run the binary with no arguments.  You will see a short command summary
 * and then the prompt:
 * @code
 *   $ ./SoDaCLI
 *   SoDaCLI -- command line interface to SoDaServer
 *     START [server-args]             -- launch server and connect
 *     SET   <target> [I|D|S] v        -- send SET (int/double/string)
 *     GET   <target>                  -- send GET
 *     REP   <target> [I|D|S] v        -- send REP
 *     AUDIO LIST                      -- list available audio devices
 *     AUDIO OUT  [device]             -- connect RX audio to speaker (muted)
 *     AUDIO VOLUME <v>                -- set output volume [0.0-1.0]
 *     AUDIO IN   [device]             -- connect mic audio to TX (muted)
 *     AUDIO GAIN <g>                  -- set input gain multiplier
 *     AUDIO TEST [device]             -- play 440 Hz tone at 0.2 amplitude (toggle)
 *     RUN   <filename>                -- execute script
 *     QUIT                            -- send STOP and exit
 *
 *   SoDaCLI>
 * @endcode
 *
 * Command verbs are case-insensitive.  Command target names (e.g.
 * @c RX_TUNE_FREQ) are converted to upper-case automatically, so you
 * may type them in any case.
 *
 * Blank lines and lines beginning with @c # are ignored, which makes
 * script files readable.
 *
 * @section sodacli_commands Commands
 *
 * @subsection sodacli_start START
 *
 * @code
 *   START [SoDaServer arguments...]
 * @endcode
 *
 * Forks and execs @c SoDaServer (found via @c PATH) with the supplied
 * arguments, then waits up to 30 seconds for the server's command socket
 * file to appear, and finally connects to it.
 *
 * The socket base name is extracted from the @c --uds_name or @c -S
 * argument if present; otherwise the SoDaServer default @c /tmp/SoDa_ is
 * used, giving a command socket at @c /tmp/SoDa__cmd.
 *
 * Example — connect to a USRP at IP address 192.168.10.2:
 * @code
 *   START --radio USRP --devargs addr=192.168.10.2 --uds_name /tmp/MySoDa_
 * @endcode
 *
 * @subsection sodacli_set SET
 *
 * @code
 *   SET <target> [I|D|S] <value>
 * @endcode
 *
 * Sends a SET command to SoDaServer.  The target name must appear in the
 * @ref SoDa::Command::CmdTarget enumeration (see @ref Command.hxx).
 *
 * The optional type indicator selects the parameter encoding:
 * - @c I — integer (enum names are also accepted, see @ref sodacli_enums)
 * - @c D — double-precision float
 * - @c S — character string
 *
 * When no type indicator is given the type is inferred from the value:
 * - Known enum names → integer
 * - Values containing @c . , @c e , or @c E → double
 * - Anything else parseable as an integer → integer
 * - Remaining tokens → string
 *
 * Examples:
 * @code
 *   SET RX_TUNE_FREQ 144215000.0
 *   SET RX_TUNE_FREQ D 144.215e6
 *   SET RX_MODE USB
 *   SET RX_MODE I USB
 *   SET TX_STATE TX_ON_1
 *   SET TX_CW_SPEED I 20
 *   SET TX_CW_TEXT S CQ
 *   SET RX_RF_GAIN D 30.0
 * @endcode
 *
 * @subsection sodacli_get GET
 *
 * @code
 *   GET <target>
 * @endcode
 *
 * Sends a GET request to SoDaServer.  The server will typically respond
 * with a REP message on the command socket, which SoDaCLI prints
 * automatically (see @ref sodacli_incoming).
 *
 * Example:
 * @code
 *   GET SDR_VERSION
 *   GET HWMB_REP
 *   GET RX_RF_GAIN
 * @endcode
 *
 * @subsection sodacli_rep REP
 *
 * @code
 *   REP <target> [I|D|S] <value>
 * @endcode
 *
 * Sends a REP (report) command.  The syntax is identical to @c SET.
 * REP is seldom needed from the CLI; it is included for completeness
 * and for test harness use.
 *
 * @subsection sodacli_run RUN
 *
 * @code
 *   RUN <filename>
 * @endcode
 *
 * Opens @c filename and processes each line as if it were typed at the
 * prompt.  The filename argument is case-sensitive on Linux.  Script
 * execution stops immediately if a line causes an error; the file is
 * then closed.
 *
 * Incoming server messages are drained after each script line.
 *
 * Example script file @c init.cli:
 * @code
 *   # Connect to USRP and configure initial state
 *   START --radio USRP --uds_name /tmp/MySoDa_
 *   SET RX_TUNE_FREQ 144215000.0
 *   SET RX_MODE USB
 *   SET RX_RF_GAIN D 30.0
 *   GET SDR_VERSION
 * @endcode
 * Run it with:
 * @code
 *   SoDaCLI> RUN init.cli
 * @endcode
 *
 * @subsection sodacli_audio AUDIO
 *
 * @code
 *   AUDIO OUT  [device]
 *   AUDIO VOLUME <v>
 *   AUDIO IN   [device]
 *   AUDIO GAIN <g>
 * @endcode
 *
 * Controls real-time audio I/O between SoDaCLI and SoDaServer.  Audio is
 * exchanged as raw 32-bit float PCM samples at 48 000 Hz, mono, over
 * Unix-domain sockets that SoDaServer creates alongside the command socket.
 *
 * Each audio direction runs in its own Qt thread with a private event loop,
 * so audio processing never blocks the REPL.
 *
 * **AUDIO LIST** — print all available audio output and input devices, one per
 * line.  The system default device for each direction is marked with an
 * asterisk.  Use the device description (or a unique substring of it) as the
 * argument to @c AUDIO @c OUT or @c AUDIO @c IN.  This command does not
 * require a server connection.
 *
 * **AUDIO OUT [device]** — start receiving demodulated audio from SoDaServer
 * and play it through the named output device.  The initial volume is 0
 * (muted).  Use @c AUDIO @c VOLUME to unmute.  If @c device is omitted or
 * @c "default", the system default output device is used.  A substring match
 * (case-insensitive) against the device description is performed, so
 * @c "Headphones" matches @c "Built-in Headphones".
 *
 * **AUDIO VOLUME v** — set playback volume to @c v, a linear floating-point
 * factor in @c [0.0, 1.0].  This command is valid only after @c AUDIO @c OUT.
 *
 * **AUDIO IN [device]** — start capturing microphone audio and forwarding it
 * to SoDaServer.  The initial gain is 0 (muted).  Use @c AUDIO @c GAIN to
 * unmute.  Device matching follows the same rules as @c AUDIO @c OUT.
 *
 * **AUDIO GAIN g** — set the capture gain multiplier to @c g.  A value of
 * @c 1.0 means unity gain; values above 1.0 amplify the captured samples.
 * This command is valid only after @c AUDIO @c IN.
 *
 * **AUDIO TEST [device]** — toggle a 440 Hz sine-wave tone at 0.2 amplitude
 * (−14 dBFS) through the named output device.  Run a second time to stop.
 * This command does not require a server connection; it is useful for verifying
 * that audio output is working before connecting to SoDaServer.  Device matching
 * follows the same rules as @c AUDIO @c OUT.  If @c device is omitted, the
 * system default output device is used.
 *
 * The server's audio socket paths are derived from the socket base name in
 * the same way as the command socket:
 * - @c {basename}_rxa — server → CLI (receive audio, use with @c AUDIO @c OUT)
 * - @c {basename}_txa — CLI → server (transmit audio, use with @c AUDIO @c IN)
 *
 * Example:
 * @code
 *   SoDaCLI> AUDIO OUT default
 *   Audio output: Built-in Audio Analog Stereo
 *   SoDaCLI> AUDIO VOLUME 0.8
 *   SoDaCLI> AUDIO IN Focusrite
 *   Audio input: Focusrite USB Audio
 *   SoDaCLI> AUDIO GAIN 1.0
 * @endcode
 *
 * @subsection sodacli_quit QUIT
 *
 * @code
 *   QUIT
 * @endcode
 *
 * Sends @c SET @c STOP to SoDaServer, waits one second for any final
 * messages, stops any active audio threads, disconnects from the socket,
 * then exits SoDaCLI.  If SoDaServer was started with the @c START command,
 * the server process is also reaped.
 *
 * @section sodacli_incoming Incoming Messages
 *
 * While waiting for user input SoDaCLI polls the command socket for
 * inbound messages from the server.  Any arriving SET, GET, or REP
 * message is pretty-printed to stdout (using
 * @ref SoDa::Command::toString()) and logged:
 *
 * @code
 *   << REP SDR_VERSION S "SoDaRadio-2.x.y"
 *   << REP HWMB_REP S "B200"
 * @endcode
 *
 * After every command sent the socket is also drained, so short
 * request-reply exchanges work naturally.
 *
 * @section sodacli_enums Parameter Types and Enum Names
 *
 * When a target takes an integer parameter drawn from a named
 * enumeration you may supply the symbolic name instead of the integer.
 * The following names are recognised:
 *
 * | Enumeration          | Names |
 * |----------------------|-------|
 * | ModulationType       | @c LSB @c USB @c CW_U @c CW_L @c AM @c WBFM @c NBFM |
 * | RxTxState            | @c TX_OFF_0 @c TX_OFF_1 @c TX_OFF_2 @c TX_ON_0 @c TX_ON_1 @c TX_ON_2 |
 * | ClockSource          | @c EXTERNAL @c INTERNAL |
 * | TXAudioSelector      | @c MIC @c NOISE |
 *
 * For targets not listed above, supply the raw integer value.
 *
 * @section sodacli_targets Command Targets
 *
 * The full list of command targets is defined in @ref SoDa::Command::CmdTarget.
 * Commonly used targets and their expected parameter types are:
 *
 * | Target             | Type   | Description |
 * |--------------------|--------|-------------|
 * | RX_TUNE_FREQ       | double | RX centre frequency (Hz) |
 * | TX_TUNE_FREQ       | double | TX centre frequency (Hz) |
 * | RX_MODE            | int    | Modulation mode (see ModulationType) |
 * | TX_MODE            | int    | TX modulation mode |
 * | RX_RF_GAIN         | double | RX front-end gain (dB) |
 * | TX_RF_GAIN         | double | TX amplifier gain (dB) |
 * | RX_AF_GAIN         | double | RX audio gain |
 * | TX_STATE           | int    | Transmit on/off state (see RxTxState) |
 * | TX_CW_SPEED        | int    | CW keyer speed (WPM) |
 * | TX_CW_TEXT         | string | CW text to send (up to 8 chars) |
 * | CLOCK_SOURCE       | int    | Reference clock (INTERNAL or EXTERNAL) |
 * | SDR_VERSION        | —      | GET only; server reports version string |
 * | HWMB_REP           | —      | GET only; server reports motherboard name |
 * | STOP               | —      | Shut down all server threads |
 *
 * @section sodacli_log Log File
 *
 * SoDaCLI appends all session activity to @c CLIHarnessLog.md in the
 * current working directory.  Each session begins with a Markdown
 * heading:
 *
 * @code
 *   # SoDaCLI Session
 * @endcode
 *
 * Log entry prefixes:
 * - @c INPUT: — a line typed by the user
 * - @c SEND: — a command transmitted to the server
 * - @c RECV: — a message received from the server
 * - @c STATUS: — a connection lifecycle event
 * - @c RUN: — a script file executed
 * - @c ERROR: — a parse or I/O error
 *
 * @section sodacli_example_session Example Session
 *
 * @code
 *   $ ./SoDaCLI
 *   SoDaCLI> START --radio USRP --devargs addr=192.168.10.2
 *   Waiting for server socket [/tmp/SoDa__cmd]...
 *   Connected to SoDaServer
 *
 *   SoDaCLI> GET SDR_VERSION
 *     >> GET SDR_VERSION
 *     << REP SDR_VERSION S "SoDaRadio-2.5.0"
 *
 *   SoDaCLI> SET RX_TUNE_FREQ 144215000.0
 *     >> SET RX_TUNE_FREQ D 1.44215e+08
 *     << REP RX_LO_FREQ D 1.44e+08
 *     << REP RX_IF_FREQ D 2.15e+04
 *
 *   SoDaCLI> SET RX_MODE USB
 *     >> SET RX_MODE I 1
 *
 *   SoDaCLI> QUIT
 *     >> SET STOP I 0
 * @endcode
 */
